// app:ui/grid — the cell-grid renderer: a screen of `cell.Cell`s, the glyph
// atlas behind them, and one instanced draw that paints the lot.
//
//     grid.up(display.window_get_dpi_scale())     // once, after sokol_gfx is set up
//     if grid.begin(fb_w, fb_h): repaint_all()    // per frame, before writing
//     grid.text(0, 0, "hello", 80, fg, bg)
//     grid.prepare()                              // per frame, outside the pass
//     grid.render(fb_w, fb_h)                     // per frame, inside the pass
//     grid.down()
//
//     grid.set_layers(2)                          // a floating layer over the base
//     grid.target(1)                              // writers now write layer 1
//     _ = grid.chrome_add(1, box)                 // free-pixel chrome under it
//
// **An engine: the mechanism is hidden and every piece of it can be read.**
// No program names a face, a slot, a texture or a buffer. The face chain is
// chosen here; printable ASCII is made resident by `up`, any other cluster
// the first time a writer meets it; the page is uploaded when it changed. Each of those has a
// reader — `stats`, `cell_w`, `cell_at`, `face_count` and the rest — so a test
// or an instrument can see what the engine did without reaching into it.
//
// ## The cell is derived from the live face
//
// The requested pixel height is `PIXEL_HEIGHT` times the display's DPI scale,
// which the caller hands to `up`.
// The primary face's advance of `'0'` at that height is `adv`; the cell is
// `cw = round(adv)` pixels wide, and the face is then rasterised at the
// pixel height that makes its advance exactly `cw` — `px = requested * cw /
// adv`, since an advance scales linearly with the pixel height. Rounding the
// face rather than the grid is the point: every glyph lands on whole pixels
// with no drift across a line. The cell is `ceil(ascent - descent + line_gap)`
// pixels tall at `px`, and the baseline `round(ascent)` pixels below its top.
// `cell_size_for`, `cell_height_for` and `baseline_for` are that arithmetic,
// pure, with the face's numbers as arguments.
//
// ## Cells are retained
//
// `begin` sizes the screen to whole cells and clears it **only when the size
// changed**, answering true so the caller repaints everything. Otherwise the
// cells a caller wrote last frame are still there; nothing clears them. Each
// drawn layer's whole buffer is still uploaded every frame — `cols * rows *
// 12` bytes a layer, which `stats().upload_bytes` sums — because one upload of
// a contiguous run is what an instanced draw wants, and tracking dirty cells
// would not save it.
//
// The grid's origin is the framebuffer's (0, 0): cell (`col`, `row`) covers
// pixels `col * cell_w()` to `(col + 1) * cell_w()` across and `row *
// cell_h()` to `(row + 1) * cell_h()` down, which is how a caller turns a
// cell rectangle into the pixel rectangle of chrome that sits under it.
//
// ## Layer groups
//
// A layer is a whole screen of cells at one depth — the base UI, a floating
// window, a tooltip — each with its own cell buffer and its own chrome
// (`chrome.dado`), drawn in layer order, each as its chrome and then its cells
// in one instanced draw. Layer 0 is `BASE`; `set_layers` says how many there
// are, up to `MAX_LAYERS`. Every writer writes the layer `target` names, and a
// target that names no layer writes nowhere. `begin` points the target back
// at `BASE`, so a paint that targets a layer and forgets to point back loses
// at most one frame of the base's writes rather than every one after it.
//
// A layer above the base starts fully transparent — every cell blank, with
// background alpha 0, which is what a zeroed `cell.Cell` is — so the layers
// below show through everywhere it was not written. Its cell buffer is not
// allocated until something writes to it, and a layer that has not been
// written since it was last cleared (`clear_layer`, or `begin` on a resize) is
// neither uploaded nor drawn; its chrome still is. The base always draws.
// Measured under Xvfb with Mesa's software GL, 2026-09-22, at 1000 x 360
// pixels of 8 x 16 cells (125 x 22 = 2750 cells): one layer uploaded 33000
// bytes and made one instanced draw; a second, written layer made that 66000
// bytes and two; the same second layer cleared went back to 33000 and one.
//
// ## The atlas upload is once a frame, outside the keystroke
//
// A writer that meets a new cluster rasterises it into its slot at once —
// the cell needs the slot number now — and only marks the page dirty. The
// upload is `prepare`'s, once per frame at most, whatever number of clusters
// arrived since the last one; it is the whole page, because `sg_update_image`
// has no partial form. Since `up` makes printable ASCII resident before the
// first upload, typing ordinary text uploads nothing; a new character of
// another script costs one upload of the page's current size, which the atlas
// keeps as small as what it holds (see `atlas.dado`). `stats` says what each
// frame sent: `frame_page_upload_bytes`.
//
// ## Memory
//
// Everything this package allocates comes from the allocator that was ambient
// when `up` ran, captured there. The writers, `begin` and `chrome_add` run
// later, inside a program's per-frame temporary arena, and a cell buffer, a
// layer's cells made at its first write, a chrome list or an atlas entry
// placed there would be rolled back under the renderer at the end of the
// frame. Before `up` nothing is allocated: a write to a layer above the base
// is dropped and `chrome_add` answers false.
//
// ## Scrolling regions
//
// The window keeps its one cell grid for layout and hit-testing. A **region**
// is a cell-aligned rectangle of that grid whose interior is drawn from a cell
// buffer of its own, shifted up by a sub-cell number of pixels and clipped to
// the rectangle, so a list or a document can scroll smoothly while every
// other cell stays on the lattice.
//
//     i32 id = grid.region_make(grid.BASE)       // once; -1 when refused
//     if grid.region_set_rect(id, r): repaint(id) // true: remade blank
//     grid.target_region(id)                     // writers now write it
//     _ = grid.text(r.col, r.row + 3, "...", r.cols, fg, bg)
//     grid.region_set_offset(id, dy)             // 0 <= dy < cell_h()
//     grid.region_scroll_rows(id, 1)             // content up a whole row
//     grid.target(grid.BASE)
//
// **The buffer is `rect.cols` x `(rect.rows + 1)` cells.** Shifted up by `dy`
// pixels, the rect's `rect.rows` rows show the bottom `cell_h() - dy` pixels
// of buffer row 0, rows 1 .. `rect.rows - 1` whole, and the top `dy` pixels
// of buffer row `rect.rows` — the spare row, one below the rect, which is
// why it exists: a partially visible bottom row always has content. A
// scroll by a pixel is therefore `region_set_offset` alone, which uploads
// nothing; when the offset would reach a whole cell the caller instead sets
// it back to 0, calls `region_scroll_rows(id, 1)` — a `memmove` of the
// buffer, not a repaint — and paints the one row it exposed. Only vertical
// pixel offsets exist: a horizontal scroll stays column-granular, done by
// repainting from a different line column (`text_from`).
//
// **Writers speak screen cells.** While a region is targeted, `put`, `fill`,
// `text` and `text_from` take the same screen coordinates as ever, and a cell
// is stored when it lies in the region's **extent** — the rect's columns,
// rows `rect.row` .. `rect.row + rect.rows` inclusive, the last being the
// spare row — and, while a clip is set, in the clip as `region_clip_for`
// extends it: a clip whose bottom edge is the rect's own bottom edge is
// grown by the spare row, since a widget clipped to the region's rect must be
// able to paint the row that scrolls into it; any other clip is used as it
// is. The screen's edge does not clip a region (its spare row may lie below
// the screen). `target(layer)` points the writers back at a layer, and so
// does `begin`.
//
// **Drawing.** `render` draws each layer as its chrome, its cells, then its
// regions in creation order, then its ink lists (`ink_make`). A region's
// buffer is uploaded only when it was written, scrolled or remade since its
// last upload — a frame that only moves the offset sends nothing — then the
// same pipeline draws it with the uniforms' grid origin at the rect's pixel
// origin less `(0, dy)` and its column count at the rect's, one instanced draw
// of `cols * (rows + 1)` cells, under a scissor of the rect's pixels, and the
// scissor goes back to the whole framebuffer. The shader is unchanged: its
// origin uniform already places the grid, and `dy` is whole pixels, so every
// corner still lands on a pixel edge and the NEAREST sampler still reads each
// texel once.
//
// **Bookkeeping.** A region's cells are not in its layer's buffer; the
// layer's own cells under the rect should be left transparent by the caller
// (a zeroed cell is), because the region draws over them and whatever they
// hold shows through the region's own transparent cells. A rect that changes
// remakes the buffer blank and `region_set_rect` answers true so the caller
// repaints. Regions survive `begin`'s resize with their buffers, but the rect
// is in cells and only the caller knows where it goes at the new size, so it
// is the caller's to set again. A region on a layer that `set_layers` has
// since dropped is kept and not drawn. An id that names no live region does
// nothing anywhere; ids are reused after `region_free`. The region table
// grows by doubling from the captured allocator, with no fixed cap.
//
// ## The ASCII fast path
//
// `text` spends most of a plain line finding clusters and looking them up:
// a UTF-8 decode and a cluster scan in `layout`, then a decode and a hashed
// table probe in `atlas_slot`, per cell. A printable ASCII byte (0x20..0x7E)
// whose next byte is also below 0x80 is a cluster of its own — no combining
// mark, variation selector or joiner is ASCII, and the only ASCII pair a
// cluster joins is CR LF, whose CR is not printable — and one column wide, so
// it goes straight to its slot through the 95-entry table `atlas_prewarm`
// fills. A tab, any other byte, and a printable byte followed by one at or
// above 0x80 take the general path, one cluster at a time, and the fast path
// resumes after it. The table is the atlas's, so a rebuilt atlas starts with
// an empty one and every byte takes the general path until it is prewarmed.
// A line written as several coloured runs (`text_span`, one call a run) takes
// the fast path on exactly the cells one `text_from` over the whole line
// would: the lookahead reads the line past the run's end, and a run resumes
// at the byte and column the last one answered.
//
// ## Headless
//
// Under `-D BACKEND=dummy` the package compiles and `up` answers false: the
// shader compiler writes no shader for sokol's dummy backend, so there is
// nothing to draw with, and `last_error` says so. The pure arithmetic and the
// atlas work the same either way; the tests use nothing else. A test that
// needs cells stored — layers, regions, a widget's paint — takes a cell size
// and the allocator with `up_headless` instead, which touches no GPU.
//
// That is why the DPI scale is an argument to `up` and not read here:
// `app:display` imports `sokol_app`, which has no dummy backend and refuses
// to compile under one, so importing it would take the headless build away.
// For the same reason `up` makes the instance buffer one cell long rather
// than sized to the window, and `render` grows it on the first frame.
package grid

import "app:font"
import "app:ui/cell"
import "core:chars"
import "core:mem"
import "vendor:sokol/sokol_gfx"
import "vendor:stb/stb_truetype"

// `#include` order is the order `foreign` blocks are met, and a header is
// included once at its first mention. The generated shader header needs
// `sokol_gfx.h` above it and says so with an `#error`, so this block names
// `sokol_gfx.h` first and declares nothing. A block with no body must carry a
// clause, and this one restates the `define` the binding already writes:
// defines are emitted once each, so it costs nothing.
foreign "sokol_gfx.h" define "SOKOL_IMPL"

foreign "./grid.glsl.h" define "SOKOL_SHDC_IMPL":
    ^const sokol_gfx.sg_shader_desc ui_grid_grid_shader_desc(sokol_gfx.sg_backend backend)

// libc's, for `region_scroll_rows`: shifting a region's rows is one
// overlapping copy, which is what `memmove` is. Marked `@macro` because a
// fortified `<string.h>` (macOS's, by default) defines it as a macro, and an
// unmarked binding of one is a build error there.
private foreign "string.h":
    @macro rawptr memmove(rawptr dst, rawptr src, u64 n)

// The requested text height in logical pixels, before the DPI scale.
@const f32 PIXEL_HEIGHT = 16.0

// The base layer, which always exists and always draws, and the most layers
// there can be. Eight is a choice: the base, a few floating windows, a menu
// and a tooltip, with room left. Each costs a screen of cells only once
// something writes to it.
const i32 BASE = 0
const i32 MAX_LAYERS = 8

// The id of the one face chain there is. A key in the atlas carries it, so a
// second chain (a bold one, say) can share the table without sharing slots.
const u32 CHAIN_ID = 1

// The candidate faces, in the order a code point tries them. The first that
// loads is the primary and sets the cell; a path that does not load is
// skipped. Linux: DejaVu Sans Mono, then DejaVu Sans (symbols and Hebrew,
// which the mono face lacks — measured: `stbtt_FindGlyphIndex` answers 0 for
// U+05D0 in DejaVu Sans Mono and 1319 in DejaVu Sans), then the Japanese
// gothic face Debian installs at that name (U+65E5 present). All three were
// checked to exist on the Linux box this was written on; on the other systems
// only `font.default_path()` is tried, and that path is unverified there.
private string8 chain_path(i32 i):
    if i == 0:
        return font.default_path()
    when #OS == #LINUX || #OS == #FREEBSD:
        if i == 1:
            return "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/dejavu/DejaVuSans.ttf"
        if i == 2:
            return "/usr/share/fonts/truetype/fonts-japanese-gothic.ttf"
    return ""

private const i32 MAX_FACES = 3

// ── Pure arithmetic ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The cell width and the pixel height to rasterise at, from the requested
// pixel height and the face's advance of `'0'` measured at that height.
// `cw = max(1, round(adv))` and `px = requested * cw / adv`, which makes the
// advance at `px` exactly `cw`. A requested height below 1 (or NaN) reads as
// 1. An advance that is not positive (or NaN) cannot be scaled to anything,
// so the answer is the requested height unchanged and a cell half of it wide,
// rounded, at least 1 — a guess, stated as one, so that a face with no `'0'`
// still draws.
(i32 cw, f32 px) cell_size_for(f32 requested, f32 adv):
    f32 r = requested
    if !(r >= 1.0):
        r = 1.0
    if !(adv > 0.0) || adv > 1000000.0:
        i32 g = i32(round_half_up(r * 0.5))
        if g < 1:
            g = 1
        return (g, r)
    i32 cw = i32(round_half_up(adv))
    if cw < 1:
        cw = 1
    return (cw, r * f32(cw) / adv)

// The cell height for a face whose ascent, descent (negative, below the
// baseline) and line gap are given in pixels at the chosen height:
// `ceil(ascent - descent + line_gap)`, at least 1.
//
// The ceiling forgives `CEIL_SLACK` of a pixel. The three metrics are integer
// design units times one `f32` scale, and `stbtt_ScaleForPixelHeight` picks
// that scale so ascent minus descent is the pixel height exactly — which in
// `f32` comes out a hair over: DejaVu Sans Mono at 16 px measured here as
// 12.7584 + 3.24161 = 16.00001, whose plain ceiling is 17, a whole blank row
// under every line. A 64th of a pixel is far above that rounding noise and
// far below anything a rasteriser can show.
i32 cell_height_for(f32 ascent, f32 descent, f32 line_gap):
    f32 h = ascent - descent + line_gap - CEIL_SLACK
    if !(h > 1.0) || h > 1000000.0:
        return 1
    i32 n = i32(h)
    if f32(n) < h:
        n = n + 1
    return n

private const f32 CEIL_SLACK = 0.015625

// The baseline's pixel row within a cell `ch` tall: `round(ascent)`, held
// inside the cell.
i32 baseline_for(f32 ascent, i32 ch):
    if !(ascent > 0.0):
        return 0
    i32 b = i32(round_half_up(ascent))
    if b > ch:
        b = ch
    return b

// Where the underline and the strike line go within a cell, and their shared
// thickness, all whole pixels. `ul_pos` is pixels below the baseline and
// `ul_thick` the thickness, as `font.underline` answers them; `x_height` is
// the face's. Thickness is `round(ul_thick)`, at least 1. The underline starts
// `round(ul_pos)` rows below the baseline and the strike line is centred on
// half the x-height above it; both are held inside the cell.
(i32 underline, i32 strike, i32 thick) lines_for(i32 baseline, i32 ch, f32 ul_pos, f32 ul_thick, f32 x_height):
    if ch < 1:
        return (0, 0, 0)
    i32 t = i32(round_half_up(ul_thick))
    if t < 1:
        t = 1
    if t > ch:
        t = ch
    // Both offsets are held to a cell either way before they become integers:
    // the answer is clamped into the cell below regardless, but a float
    // becoming an `i32` saturates, and `baseline` plus or minus a saturated
    // offset is an overflow and a trap. NaN stays NaN and converts to 0.
    f32 span = f32(ch)
    f32 pos = ul_pos
    if pos > span: pos = span
    if pos < 0.0 - span: pos = 0.0 - span
    f32 half_x = x_height * 0.5
    if half_x > span: half_x = span
    if half_x < 0.0 - span: half_x = 0.0 - span
    i32 u = baseline + i32(round_half_up(pos))
    if u + t > ch:
        u = ch - t
    if u < 0:
        u = 0
    i32 s = baseline - i32(round_half_up(half_x)) - t / 2
    if s + t > ch:
        s = ch - t
    if s < 0:
        s = 0
    return (u, s, t)

// The screen in whole cells for a framebuffer `fb_w` x `fb_h`: a partial cell
// at the right or bottom edge is not drawn. Zero either way for a
// non-positive framebuffer or cell.
(i32 cols, i32 rows) grid_size_for(i32 fb_w, i32 fb_h, i32 cw, i32 ch):
    if fb_w < 1 || fb_h < 1 || cw < 1 || ch < 1:
        return (0, 0)
    return (fb_w / cw, fb_h / ch)

// What one cluster of a laid-out string becomes. `Glyph` is drawn from the
// atlas across `cols` cells (2 for a wide cluster); `Blank` is `cols` blank
// cells — a tab, or a wide cluster that would straddle the column limit.
enum SpanKind: (
    Glyph = 0,
    Blank = 1,
)

// One cluster: bytes `at` up to `end`, starting at column `col` (counted from
// the start of the string, where tab stops are measured from) and taking
// `cols` columns.
type Span: (i32 at, i32 end, i32 col, i32 cols, SpanKind kind)

// Lay out `s` from byte `at`, which sits at column `col`, up to column `limit`,
// one `Span` per cluster into `out`; answers how many were written. It stops
// at the end of `s`, at `limit`, or when `out` is full, and a caller resumes
// from the last span's `end` and `col + cols`. Every span written makes
// progress, so a resume loop that stops on zero ends.
//
// The rules: a tab reaches the next stop (`cell.cluster_columns_at`) and is
// blank, cut short at `limit`; a wide cluster whose second column would pass
// `limit` becomes one blank column; any other cluster — a control included,
// which the atlas draws as U+FFFD — is a glyph of the columns `cell` gives it.
// Combining marks ride in their base's span and take no column of their own.
i32 layout(string8 s, i32 at, i32 col, i32 limit, []Span out):
    i32 n = i32(#len(s))
    i32 room = i32(#len(out))
    if at < 0:
        at = 0
    if col < 0:
        col = 0
    i32 k = 0
    for at < n && col < limit && k < room:
        i32 end = chars.cluster_end(s, at)
        i32 w = cell.cluster_columns_at(s, at, col)
        Span sp = (at, end, col, w, SpanKind.Glyph)
        if s[at] == '\t':
            sp.kind = SpanKind.Blank
            if col + w > limit:
                sp.cols = limit - col
        else if w == 2 && col + 2 > limit:
            sp.kind = SpanKind.Blank
            sp.cols = 1
        out[k] = sp
        k = k + 1
        at = end
        col = col + sp.cols
    return k

// ── State ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

private bool g_up
private (rawptr(rawptr, AllocatorMode, u64, u64, rawptr, u64) proc, rawptr data) g_alloc
private string8 g_error

private [3]Face g_faces
private i32 g_nfaces
private [3]string8 g_face_paths

private f32 g_requested
private f32 g_px
private i32 g_cw
private i32 g_ch
private i32 g_baseline
private i32 g_ul_row
private i32 g_strike_row
private i32 g_line_thick

private Atlas g_atlas
private bool g_atlas_made

// One layer: its cells (nil until the first write, except the base's, which
// `begin` makes), whether anything has been written since it was last
// cleared, its chrome, and its instance buffer on the GPU. sokol_gfx allows
// one update of a buffer per frame, so each layer has its own.
private type CellLayer: (
    ref []cell.Cell cells,
    bool touched,
    ChromeList chrome,
    sokol_gfx.sg_buffer buf,
    i32 buf_cells,
    bool buf_sent,
)

private [MAX_LAYERS]CellLayer g_layers
private i32 g_nlayers = 1
private i32 g_target = 0
// Whether `g_alloc` has been captured: `up` sets it, and nothing may allocate
// before.
private bool g_have_alloc
private i32 g_cols
private i32 g_rows

private sokol_gfx.sg_image g_img
// The page image's size on the GPU. It trails the atlas's page by at most a
// frame: the page grows at a lookup, the image is remade to match at the next
// `prepare`.
private i32 g_img_w
private i32 g_img_h
// The last page size the device refused an image for (`prepare`); 0 x 0 for
// none.
private i32 g_refused_w
private i32 g_refused_h
private sokol_gfx.sg_view g_view
private sokol_gfx.sg_sampler g_smp
private sokol_gfx.sg_shader g_shd
private sokol_gfx.sg_pipeline g_pip

// sokol_gfx allows one update of an image, and of a buffer, per frame. This
// and each layer's `buf_sent` are cleared by `begin`, so each is updated at
// most once between two `begin`s however often `prepare` and `render` are
// called.
private bool g_page_sent

private i32 g_cells_written
private i64 g_upload_bytes
private i32 g_draw_calls
private i32 g_layers_drawn
private i32 g_chrome_tris
private i32 g_chrome_failures
private i32 g_chrome_dropped
private i32 g_chrome_flushes
// Chrome triangles handed to sokol_gp since the last `begin`, across layers
// and `render`s: what `CHROME_FRAME_PERCENT` is counted against.
private i32 g_chrome_frame_used
private i32 g_page_uploads
private i64 g_page_upload_bytes
private i64 g_frame_page_bytes
private i32 g_region_draws
private i32 g_region_uploads
private i64 g_region_upload_bytes
private i64 g_region_rows_shifted
private i32 g_ascii_cells
// Cells written to the base layer (or a region drawn with it) since `begin`,
// counted by the run rather than by the cell: `g_cells_written` as it stood
// when the writers were last pointed somewhere (`g_seg_from`), and whether
// that somewhere is the base (`g_seg_base`); each switch of target adds the
// run just ended. `poke` — the hottest function here — pays nothing for it.
private i32 g_base_cells
private i32 g_seg_from
private bool g_seg_base = true

// One scrolling region: whether its id is live, the layer it draws with, its
// rect in screen cells and its pixel offset, its cells (`rect.cols` x
// `(rect.rows + 1)`, nil while the rect is empty), whether they changed since
// they were last uploaded, its instance buffer, and its own counts since it
// was made.
private type Region: (
    bool live,
    i32 layer,
    cell.CellRect rect,
    i32 dy,
    ref []cell.Cell cells,
    bool dirty,
    sokol_gfx.sg_buffer buf,
    i32 buf_cells,
    bool buf_sent,
    i32 uploads,
    i64 upload_bytes,
    i64 rows_shifted,
    i64 bytes_moved,
)

// The region table, indexed by id, and the live ids in creation order (the
// order `render` draws them in). Both grow together by doubling from the
// captured allocator; `g_region_live` of the order are used.
private ref []Region g_regions
private ref []i32 g_region_order
private i32 g_region_live
// Whether the writers write a region (`g_target_region`) rather than the
// layer `g_target` names.
private bool g_region_targeted
private i32 g_target_region

// The widest and tallest rect a region takes, in cells: a choice, past any
// screen (4096 columns of an 8-pixel cell is 32768 pixels), and small enough
// that a buffer's cell count, 4096 x 4097, and its pixel extent stay far
// inside `i32`. A rect past it is taken as empty.
const i32 MAX_REGION_SIDE = 4096

// ── Readers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

type Stats: (
    // Cells stored by the writers since the last `begin`.
    i32 cells_written,
    // Instance bytes the last `render` uploaded, summed over the layers it
    // uploaded, and its instanced draw calls, one per layer drawn. sokol_gp's
    // draws for the chrome are not in `draw_calls`.
    i64 upload_bytes,
    i32 draw_calls,
    i32 atlas_pages,
    i32 resident_clusters,
    // Page uploads and their bytes since `up`.
    i32 page_uploads,
    i64 page_upload_bytes,
    // Texels of ink dropped at a slot's edge since `up`.
    i32 clipped_px,
    // Lookups that found the page full and answered `.notdef`.
    i32 overflows,
    // Page bytes uploaded since the last `begin`: 0 on a frame that uploaded
    // nothing, else the page's width times its height. Read before the next
    // `begin` (a program's own process hook runs before the framework's), it
    // is what the previous frame sent.
    i64 frame_page_upload_bytes,
    // Times the page has grown a rung since `up`.
    i32 page_grows,
    // Layers whose cells the last `render` drew (the base, and every layer
    // written since its last clear).
    i32 layers_drawn,
    // Chrome triangles the last `render` handed sokol_gp, all layers; three
    // vertices each of sokol_gp's per-frame budget.
    i32 chrome_triangles,
    // Layers whose chrome the last `render` could not draw: sokol_gp not set
    // up, or a flush it refused.
    i32 chrome_failures,
    // Chrome boxes the last `render` dropped for want of budget or to a
    // refused flush — each layer's last ones in list order — and the
    // sokol_gp flushes its chrome took.
    i32 chrome_dropped,
    i32 chrome_flushes,
    // Region draws the last `render` made (one instanced draw each; not in
    // `draw_calls`), the region buffers it uploaded and their bytes (not in
    // `upload_bytes`): 0 and 0 on a frame that only moved offsets.
    i32 region_draws,
    i32 region_uploads,
    i64 region_upload_bytes,
    // Rows `region_scroll_rows` shifted since the last `begin`, all regions.
    i64 region_rows_shifted,
    // Cells the writers stored through the ASCII fast path since the last
    // `begin`: a part of `cells_written`.
    i32 ascii_cells,
    // Cells stored into the base layer, or into a region drawn with it, since
    // the last `begin`: the part of `cells_written` under every floating
    // layer, which an overlay opening or closing over it should not touch.
    i32 base_cells,
)

// The base's count with the run still open added, when it is the base's.
private i32 base_cells_now():
    if g_seg_base:
        return g_base_cells + (g_cells_written - g_seg_from)
    return g_base_cells

Stats stats():
    i32 pages = 0
    if g_atlas_made && g_atlas.page.pixels != nil:
        pages = 1
    Stats s = (g_cells_written, g_upload_bytes, g_draw_calls, pages, g_atlas.table.count,
               g_page_uploads, g_page_upload_bytes, g_atlas.clipped, g_atlas.overflows,
               g_frame_page_bytes, g_atlas.page.grows, g_layers_drawn, g_chrome_tris,
               g_chrome_failures, g_chrome_dropped, g_chrome_flushes, g_region_draws,
               g_region_uploads, g_region_upload_bytes, g_region_rows_shifted, g_ascii_cells, base_cells_now())
    return s

bool is_up():
    return g_up

string8 last_error():
    return g_error

// The cell in framebuffer pixels; 0 before `up`.
i32 cell_w():
    return g_cw

i32 cell_h():
    return g_ch

// The baseline's pixel row within a cell, and the pixel height the chain is
// rasterised at (the derived `px`, not the requested one).
i32 baseline():
    return g_baseline

f32 pixel_height():
    return g_px

f32 requested_height():
    return g_requested

// The underline row, the strike row and their thickness, within a cell.
(i32 underline, i32 strike, i32 thick) lines():
    return (g_ul_row, g_strike_row, g_line_thick)

// The screen in cells, as the last `begin` sized it.
i32 cols():
    return g_cols

i32 rows():
    return g_rows

// The cell at (`col`, `row`) of the base layer; off-screen answers the blank
// cell.
cell.Cell cell_at(i32 col, i32 row):
    return cell_at_layer(BASE, col, row)

// The cell at (`col`, `row`) of `layer`; off-screen, a layer that does not
// exist and one never written answer the blank cell.
cell.Cell cell_at_layer(i32 layer, i32 col, i32 row):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers || col < 0 || row < 0 || col >= g_cols || row >= g_rows:
        return cell.blank()
    if g_layers[layer].cells == nil:
        return cell.blank()
    return g_layers[layer].cells[i64(row) * i64(g_cols) + i64(col)]

// ── Layers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Have `n` layers, held to 1..`MAX_LAYERS`. Layers dropped by a smaller `n`
// give back their cells, their chrome and their buffer; layers added start
// transparent and allocate nothing until written. The target is left alone,
// so a target past the new count writes nowhere.
void set_layers(i32 n):
    if n < 1:
        n = 1
    if n > MAX_LAYERS:
        n = MAX_LAYERS
    for i in n..<MAX_LAYERS:
        release_layer(i32(i))
    g_nlayers = n

i32 layers():
    return g_nlayers

// Point every writer at `layer`. One that does not exist is accepted and
// writes nowhere.
void target(i32 layer):
    seg_switch(layer == BASE)
    g_target = layer
    g_region_targeted = false

i32 targeted():
    return g_target

// Make `layer` fully transparent again — every cell blank with background
// alpha 0 — and not drawn until written. Its chrome is kept (`chrome_clear`).
void clear_layer(i32 layer):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers:
        return
    ^CellLayer l = &g_layers[layer]
    if l.cells != nil:
        cell.Cell none = cell.blank()
        for i in 0..<#len(l.cells):
            l.cells[i] = none
    l.touched = false

// Whether `layer` has been written since it was last cleared. The base
// answers it like any other, though it draws either way.
bool layer_touched(i32 layer):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers:
        return false
    return g_layers[layer].touched

// Whether a layer's cells are uploaded and drawn: the base always; any other
// only when written since its last clear.
bool layer_draws(i32 layer, bool touched):
    return layer == BASE || touched

// Forget `layer`'s chrome.
void chrome_clear(i32 layer):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers:
        return
    chrome_list_clear(&g_layers[layer].chrome)

// Add `b` to `layer`'s chrome, after what is there. Answers false for a layer
// that does not exist, before `up` (whose allocator the list must live in),
// and when the list could not grow.
bool chrome_add(i32 layer, ChromeBox b):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers || !g_have_alloc:
        return false
    return chrome_list_add(&g_layers[layer].chrome, b, g_alloc)

i32 chrome_count(i32 layer):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers:
        return 0
    return g_layers[layer].chrome.n

// The `i`th box of `layer`'s chrome; a zero box off the end.
ChromeBox chrome_at(i32 layer, i32 i):
    ChromeBox none
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers || i < 0 || i >= g_layers[layer].chrome.n:
        return none
    return g_layers[layer].chrome.boxes[i]

// Give back one layer's cells, chrome and buffer. The buffer is destroyed
// only when it was made, so a layer that never drew calls no sokol function.
private void release_layer(i32 i):
    ^CellLayer l = &g_layers[i]
    if l.cells != nil:
        #delete(l.cells)
        l.cells = nil
    l.touched = false
    l.buf_sent = false
    chrome_list_free(&l.chrome)
    if l.buf.id != 0:
        if sokol_gfx.sg_isvalid():
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_buffer(l.buf)
        l.buf.id = 0
    l.buf_cells = 0

// The faces of the chain that loaded, primary first, and their paths.
i32 face_count():
    return g_nfaces

string8 face_path(i32 i):
    if i < 0 || i >= g_nfaces:
        return ""
    return g_face_paths[i]

// The pixel row within a cell where face `i` stands; -1 for no such face.
i32 face_baseline_at(i32 i):
    if i < 0 || i >= g_nfaces:
        return -1
    return g_faces[i].baseline

// Slots across one page row and the page's capacity in slots; slots handed
// out; whether the page awaits upload.
i32 atlas_across():
    return g_atlas.slots.across

i32 atlas_capacity():
    return g_atlas.slots.capacity

i32 atlas_slots_used():
    return slots_used(&g_atlas.slots)

bool atlas_dirty():
    return g_atlas.page.dirty

// The atlas page's size in pixels now, and the height it may grow to. Zero
// before `up`.
i32 atlas_page_w():
    return g_atlas.page.w

i32 atlas_page_h():
    return g_atlas.page.h

i32 atlas_page_max_h():
    return g_atlas.page.max_h

// ── Bringing it up and down ─────────────────────────────────────────────────

private bool fail(string8 why):
    down()
    g_error = why
    return false

private bool valid_image(sokol_gfx.sg_image x):
    return sokol_gfx.sg_query_image_state(x) == sokol_gfx.sg_resource_state.SG_RESOURCESTATE_VALID

// Load the face chain, derive the cell, make the atlas and the GPU objects.
// Idempotent: a second call while up answers true and does nothing, whatever
// `dpi_scale` it is given. Call it once sokol_gfx is set up — a program's
// ready hook, with `display.window_get_dpi_scale()` — under the allocator
// that should hold everything this package will ever allocate. A scale that
// is not positive (or NaN) reads as 1.
bool up(f32 dpi_scale):
    if g_up:
        return true
    g_error = ""
    if !sokol_gfx.sg_isvalid():
        return fail("grid: sokol_gfx is not set up")
    g_alloc = #default
    g_have_alloc = true

    f32 dpi = dpi_scale
    if !(dpi > 0.0):
        dpi = 1.0
    g_requested = PIXEL_HEIGHT * dpi

    g_nfaces = 0
    for i in 0..<MAX_FACES:
        string8 path = chain_path(i32(i))
        if path == "":
            continue
        (font.Font f, bool ok) = font.load_file(path, g_requested)
        if !ok || f.info == nil:
            font.destroy(&f)
            continue
        g_faces[g_nfaces].f = f
        g_face_paths[g_nfaces] = path
        g_nfaces = g_nfaces + 1
    if g_nfaces == 0:
        return fail("grid: no face in the chain loaded")

    ^font.Font primary = &g_faces[0].f
    (i32 cw, f32 px) = cell_size_for(g_requested, measured_zero(primary, g_requested))
    g_cw = cw
    g_px = px
    // Ascent, descent and gap at `px`: `app:font` measured them at the
    // requested height, and every vertical metric scales by `px / requested`.
    f32 k = g_px / font.pixel_height(primary)
    g_ch = cell_height_for(font.ascent(primary) * k, font.descent(primary) * k, primary.line_gap_px * k)
    g_baseline = baseline_for(font.ascent(primary) * k, g_ch)
    (f32 ulp, f32 ult, bool _) = font.underline(primary)
    (i32 u, i32 s, i32 t) = lines_for(g_baseline, g_ch, ulp * k, ult * k, font.x_height(primary) * k)
    g_ul_row = u
    g_strike_row = s
    g_line_thick = t
    for i in 0..<g_nfaces:
        g_faces[i].scale = stb_scale(&g_faces[i].f, g_px)
        g_faces[i].baseline = face_baseline(&g_faces[i].f, g_faces[i].scale, g_ch)

    // The device's largest texture side bounds the page ladder, so a grown
    // page is never an image the backend refuses.
    sokol_gfx.sg_limits lim = sokol_gfx.sg_query_limits()
    g_atlas = atlas_make(g_cw, g_ch, g_px, CHAIN_ID, lim.max_image_size_2d)
    g_atlas_made = true
    if g_atlas.page.pixels == nil:
        return fail("grid: the atlas page could not be allocated")
    atlas_ink_notdef(&g_atlas, g_faces[0..<g_nfaces])
    _ = atlas_prewarm(&g_atlas, g_faces[0..<g_nfaces])

    ^const sokol_gfx.sg_shader_desc sd = ui_grid_grid_shader_desc(sokol_gfx.sg_query_backend())
    if sd == nil:
        return fail("grid: no shader for this sokol_gfx backend")
    g_shd = sokol_gfx.sg_make_shader(sd)

    _ = make_page_image(g_atlas.page.w, g_atlas.page.h)

    // NEAREST: a cell origin is a whole pixel and a slot is exactly one cell,
    // so every fragment lands on one texel and nothing should blend.
    sokol_gfx.sg_sampler_desc smd
    smd.min_filter = sokol_gfx.sg_filter.SG_FILTER_NEAREST
    smd.mag_filter = sokol_gfx.sg_filter.SG_FILTER_NEAREST
    smd.wrap_u = sokol_gfx.sg_wrap.SG_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE
    smd.wrap_v = sokol_gfx.sg_wrap.SG_WRAP_CLAMP_TO_EDGE
    smd.label = "app:ui/grid sampler"
    g_smp = sokol_gfx.sg_make_sampler(&smd)

    // One per-instance buffer, stride `cell.CELL_BYTES`: `slot` and `flags`
    // read together as one USHORT2 (a `uvec2`), `fg` and `bg` as UBYTE4N (a
    // normalised `vec4`). No vertex buffer and no index buffer. The fragment
    // shader writes premultiplied colour, so "over" is ONE and
    // ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA on colour and alpha alike.
    sokol_gfx.sg_pipeline_desc pd
    pd.shader = g_shd
    pd.layout.buffers[0].stride = cell.CELL_BYTES
    pd.layout.buffers[0].step_func = sokol_gfx.sg_vertex_step.SG_VERTEXSTEP_PER_INSTANCE
    pd.layout.attrs[0].buffer_index = 0
    pd.layout.attrs[0].offset = 0
    pd.layout.attrs[0].format = sokol_gfx.sg_vertex_format.SG_VERTEXFORMAT_USHORT2
    pd.layout.attrs[1].buffer_index = 0
    pd.layout.attrs[1].offset = 4
    pd.layout.attrs[1].format = sokol_gfx.sg_vertex_format.SG_VERTEXFORMAT_UBYTE4N
    pd.layout.attrs[2].buffer_index = 0
    pd.layout.attrs[2].offset = 8
    pd.layout.attrs[2].format = sokol_gfx.sg_vertex_format.SG_VERTEXFORMAT_UBYTE4N
    pd.colors[0].blend.enabled = true
    pd.colors[0].blend.src_factor_rgb = sokol_gfx.sg_blend_factor.SG_BLENDFACTOR_ONE
    pd.colors[0].blend.dst_factor_rgb = sokol_gfx.sg_blend_factor.SG_BLENDFACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA
    pd.colors[0].blend.src_factor_alpha = sokol_gfx.sg_blend_factor.SG_BLENDFACTOR_ONE
    pd.colors[0].blend.dst_factor_alpha = sokol_gfx.sg_blend_factor.SG_BLENDFACTOR_ONE_MINUS_SRC_ALPHA
    pd.label = "app:ui/grid pipeline"
    g_pip = sokol_gfx.sg_make_pipeline(&pd)

    if !make_buffer(&g_layers[BASE], 1):
        return fail("grid: the instance buffer could not be made")

    sokol_gfx.sg_resource_state valid = sokol_gfx.sg_resource_state.SG_RESOURCESTATE_VALID
    if sokol_gfx.sg_query_shader_state(g_shd) != valid:
        return fail("grid: the shader did not compile")
    if sokol_gfx.sg_query_pipeline_state(g_pip) != valid:
        return fail("grid: the pipeline was refused")
    if !valid_image(g_img) || sokol_gfx.sg_query_view_state(g_view) != valid:
        return fail("grid: the atlas page image was refused")
    if sokol_gfx.sg_query_sampler_state(g_smp) != valid:
        return fail("grid: the sampler was refused")
    g_up = true
    return true

// The advance of `'0'` in `f` at `height` pixels, from the face's own
// horizontal metrics; 0 when the face has no `'0'` or no TrueType data.
private f32 measured_zero(^font.Font f, f32 height):
    if f.info == nil:
        return 0.0
    i32 gi = stb_truetype.stbtt_FindGlyphIndex(f.info, 48)
    if gi == 0:
        return 0.0
    i32 adv = 0
    i32 lsb = 0
    stb_truetype.stbtt_GetGlyphHMetrics(f.info, gi, &adv, &lsb)
    return f32(adv) * stb_scale(f, height)

// (Re)make the page image and its view at `w` x `h`. The new pair is made
// first and the old one destroyed only when the new one is valid, so a
// refused image leaves the old pair bound; answers whether it was made.
private bool make_page_image(i32 w, i32 h):
    sokol_gfx.sg_image_desc idesc
    idesc.type_ = sokol_gfx.sg_image_type.SG_IMAGETYPE_2D
    idesc.usage.dynamic_update = true
    idesc.width = w
    idesc.height = h
    idesc.num_slices = 1
    idesc.num_mipmaps = 1
    idesc.pixel_format = sokol_gfx.sg_pixel_format.SG_PIXELFORMAT_R8
    idesc.sample_count = 1
    idesc.label = "app:ui/grid page"
    sokol_gfx.sg_image img = sokol_gfx.sg_make_image(&idesc)
    sokol_gfx.sg_view_desc vdesc
    vdesc.texture.image = img
    sokol_gfx.sg_view vw = sokol_gfx.sg_make_view(&vdesc)
    bool ok = valid_image(img) && sokol_gfx.sg_query_view_state(vw) == sokol_gfx.sg_resource_state.SG_RESOURCESTATE_VALID
    if !ok && g_img.id != 0:
        sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_view(vw)
        sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_image(img)
        return false
    if g_view.id != 0:
        sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_view(g_view)
    if g_img.id != 0:
        sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_image(g_img)
    g_img = img
    g_view = vw
    g_img_w = w
    g_img_h = h
    return ok

// (Re)make a layer's instance buffer with room for `n` cells, at least one.
// The old buffer, if any, is destroyed first.
private bool make_buffer(^CellLayer l, i32 n):
    return make_instances(&l.buf, &l.buf_cells, n, true)

// (Re)make an instance buffer `buf` with room for `n` cells, at least one,
// recording the room in `room`. `stream` picks sokol's usage: a layer's
// buffer is replaced every frame (stream), a region's only when it changed
// (dynamic, which is what sokol_gfx asks of a buffer updated now and then).
private bool make_instances(^sokol_gfx.sg_buffer buf, ^i32 room, i32 n, bool stream):
    if n < 1:
        n = 1
    if buf.id != 0:
        sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_buffer(buf^)
        buf.id = 0
        room^ = 0
    sokol_gfx.sg_buffer_desc bd
    bd.size = #c.size_t(u64(n) * u64(cell.CELL_BYTES))
    bd.usage.vertex_buffer = true
    if stream:
        bd.usage.stream_update = true
    else:
        bd.usage.dynamic_update = true
    bd.label = "app:ui/grid cells"
    buf^ = sokol_gfx.sg_make_buffer(&bd)
    if sokol_gfx.sg_query_buffer_state(buf^) != sokol_gfx.sg_resource_state.SG_RESOURCESTATE_VALID:
        return false
    room^ = n
    return true

// Give back everything: GPU objects, the atlas, the faces, the cells. Safe
// before `up`, after a failed `up`, and twice.
void down():
    for i in 0..<MAX_LAYERS:
        release_layer(i32(i))
    release_regions()
    release_inks()
    g_nlayers = 1
    g_target = BASE
    if sokol_gfx.sg_isvalid():
        if g_pip.id != 0:
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_pipeline(g_pip)
        if g_shd.id != 0:
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_shader(g_shd)
        if g_smp.id != 0:
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_sampler(g_smp)
        if g_view.id != 0:
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_view(g_view)
        if g_img.id != 0:
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_image(g_img)
    g_pip.id = 0
    g_shd.id = 0
    g_smp.id = 0
    g_view.id = 0
    g_img.id = 0
    g_img_w = 0
    g_img_h = 0
    g_refused_w = 0
    g_refused_h = 0
    if g_atlas_made:
        atlas_free(&g_atlas)
        g_atlas_made = false
    Atlas none
    g_atlas = none
    for i in 0..<g_nfaces:
        font.destroy(&g_faces[i].f)
        g_face_paths[i] = ""
    g_nfaces = 0
    g_cols = 0
    g_rows = 0
    g_cw = 0
    g_ch = 0
    g_px = 0.0
    g_baseline = 0
    g_up = false
    g_have_alloc = false

// Take a cell of `cw` x `ch` pixels and the ambient allocator, as `up` does,
// with no GPU, no face and no atlas: for a test that drives a widget tree
// headless and needs a real cell height, layers and regions. The writers,
// `begin`, the layers and the regions then work on cells as they do when up,
// except that every cluster is stored as the blank slot (no atlas holds a
// glyph); `is_up` stays false, so `prepare` and `render` still do nothing.
// Answers false, changing nothing, while up and for a side below 1. `down`
// undoes it.
bool up_headless(i32 cw, i32 ch):
    if g_up || cw < 1 || ch < 1:
        return false
    g_alloc = #default
    g_have_alloc = true
    g_cw = cw
    g_ch = ch
    return true

// ── Per frame ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// Size the screen for a framebuffer `fb_w` x `fb_h`, and point the writers at
// `BASE`. Answers true when the size in cells changed: every layer's cells are
// then given back and remade blank — the base's here, the others at their
// next write — every layer counts as unwritten, and the caller must repaint
// everything. Otherwise every layer's cells are kept as they were. Chrome is
// in pixels and is kept either way. Before `up` the screen is 0 x 0.
bool begin(i32 fb_w, i32 fb_h):
    g_cells_written = 0
    g_page_sent = false
    for i in 0..<MAX_LAYERS:
        g_layers[i].buf_sent = false
    g_frame_page_bytes = 0
    g_chrome_frame_used = 0
    g_target = BASE
    g_region_targeted = false
    g_region_rows_shifted = 0
    g_ascii_cells = 0
    g_base_cells = 0
    g_seg_from = 0
    g_seg_base = true
    for i in 0..<region_capacity():
        g_regions[i].buf_sent = false
    (i32 c, i32 r) = grid_size_for(fb_w, fb_h, g_cw, g_ch)
    if c == g_cols && r == g_rows && (g_layers[BASE].cells != nil || c * r == 0):
        return false
    for i in 0..<MAX_LAYERS:
        ^CellLayer l = &g_layers[i]
        if l.cells != nil:
            #delete(l.cells)
            l.cells = nil
        l.touched = false
    g_cols = 0
    g_rows = 0
    if c * r > 0:
        g_layers[BASE].cells = #make([]cell.Cell, i64(c) * i64(r), g_alloc)
        if g_layers[BASE].cells == nil:
            return true
        g_cols = c
        g_rows = r
    return true

// A rectangle every writer is further clipped to, while `g_clipping` is set.
// A widget's paint sets it to the rectangle it was given, so text laid out
// from the widget's own edge cannot land in cells an ancestor cut away.
private cell.CellRect g_clip
private bool g_clipping

// Clip every writer to `r` (as well as to the screen) until `unclip`.
void clip(cell.CellRect r):
    g_clip = r
    g_clipping = true

void unclip():
    g_clipping = false

// Store one cell in the targeted layer, clipped to the screen and to the
// current clip. A layer above the base gets its cells here, at its first
// write, from the captured allocator.
private void poke(i32 col, i32 row, u16 slot, u16 flags, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    if g_region_targeted:
        poke_region(col, row, slot, flags, fg, bg)
        return
    if g_target < 0 || g_target >= g_nlayers:
        return
    if col < 0 || row < 0 || col >= g_cols || row >= g_rows:
        return
    if g_clipping && !cell.contains(g_clip, col, row):
        return
    ^CellLayer l = &g_layers[g_target]
    if l.cells == nil:
        if !g_have_alloc:
            return
        l.cells = #make([]cell.Cell, i64(g_cols) * i64(g_rows), g_alloc)
        if l.cells == nil:
            return
    l.touched = true
    ^cell.Cell c = &l.cells[i64(row) * i64(g_cols) + i64(col)]
    c.slot = slot
    c.flags = flags
    c.fg = fg
    c.bg = bg
    g_cells_written = g_cells_written + 1

// Store one cell in the targeted region, at screen cell (`col`, `row`),
// clipped to the region's extent and to the current clip as
// `region_clip_for` extends it.
private void poke_region(i32 col, i32 row, u16 slot, u16 flags, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    if !region_valid(g_target_region):
        return
    ^Region g = &g_regions[g_target_region]
    if g.cells == nil || !cell.contains(region_extent(g.rect), col, row):
        return
    if g_clipping && !cell.contains(region_clip_for(g_clip, g.rect), col, row):
        return
    ^cell.Cell c = &g.cells[i64(row - g.rect.row) * i64(g.rect.cols) + i64(col - g.rect.col)]
    c.slot = slot
    c.flags = flags
    c.fg = fg
    c.bg = bg
    g.dirty = true
    g_cells_written = g_cells_written + 1

// Store `c` at (`col`, `row`); off-screen is ignored.
void put(i32 col, i32 row, cell.Cell c):
    poke(col, row, c.slot, c.flags, c.fg, c.bg)

// Blank every cell of `r` on the screen, in the given colours — or, while a
// region is targeted, of `r` within the region's extent.
void fill(cell.CellRect r, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    cell.CellRect bound = cell.rect(0, 0, g_cols, g_rows)
    if g_region_targeted:
        if !region_valid(g_target_region):
            return
        bound = region_extent(g_regions[g_target_region].rect)
    cell.CellRect on = cell.intersect(r, bound)
    if cell.is_empty(on):
        return
    for y in on.row..<(on.row + on.rows):
        for x in on.col..<(on.col + on.cols):
            poke(x, y, BLANK_SLOT, 0, fg, bg)

// The atlas slot of the cluster at bytes `at` up to `end` of `s`, made
// resident if it was not; for a wide cluster, the left slot (the right is one
// more). A range that is empty, out of bounds or inverted, a tab, and any call
// before `up` answer the blank slot.
u16 slot_of(string8 s, i32 at, i32 end):
    i32 n = i32(#len(s))
    if !g_up || at < 0 || end > n || at >= end:
        return BLANK_SLOT
    string8 c = s[at..<end]
    return atlas_slot(&g_atlas, g_faces[0..<g_nfaces], c, cell.cluster_columns_at(c, 0, 0))

// Write `s` from (`col`, `row`) in at most `max_cols` columns, one cluster per
// cell (two for a wide one), by `layout`'s rules; answers the columns laid
// out, which does not depend on where the screen ends — cells past its edge
// are dropped, not refused. A wide cluster's left cell carries
// `cell.WIDE_LEFT` and its right cell `cell.WIDE_RIGHT` and the slot beside
// the left one, unless the atlas answered `.notdef` for a full page, in which
// case the right cell is blank. `max_cols` at or below zero writes nothing.
i32 text(i32 col, i32 row, string8 s, i32 max_cols, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    return text_from(col, row, s, 0, 0, max_cols, fg, bg)

// Like `text`, but starting part-way into a line: the cluster at byte `at`
// sits at line column `at_col`, and line column `at_col` lands on screen column
// `col`. Tab stops are counted from the line's own column 0, so a line drawn
// scrolled left keeps its tabs where they were. Answers the line column after
// the last one laid out.
//
// A run of printable ASCII takes the fast path (see the file's head): each
// byte whose next byte is below 0x80 is stored with the slot `atlas_prewarm`
// recorded for it, one column, with no decode, no cluster scan and no table
// probe. Everything else is laid out by `layout` one cluster at a time, so the
// fast path resumes right after it. `layout` holds a negative starting column
// at 0, and so does this, which is why `c` starts there.
i32 text_from(i32 col, i32 row, string8 s, i32 at, i32 at_col, i32 max_cols, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    (i32 _, i32 c) = text_span(col, row, s, at, i32(#len(s)), at_col, max_cols, fg, bg)
    return c

// `text_from` for the clusters that begin before byte `end` only — one styled
// run of a line, so a line in several colours is written as consecutive runs,
// each resuming where the last stopped. Answers the byte and the line column
// after the last cluster laid out (`at` and `at_col` when nothing was): the
// next run's `at` and `at_col`. A cluster that begins before `end` is laid
// out whole, so the byte answered can pass `end`; the caller's next run then
// starts there. Every cluster is still read against all of `s`, so a run
// that stops at `end` takes the ASCII fast path exactly where one call over
// the whole line would: the lookahead that decides it reads the byte after,
// wherever `end` is. An `end` past the string is its length.
(i32 at, i32 col) text_span(i32 col, i32 row, string8 s, i32 at, i32 end, i32 at_col, i32 max_cols, [4]u8 fg, [4]u8 bg):
    if max_cols <= 0:
        return (at, at_col)
    // One span is used. Two are declared because `dadoc` types a slice of a
    // one-element array of records as a slice of its first field's type
    // (ERR0504 at the call: "[]Span ... and this is []i32").
    [2]Span spans
    i32 n = i32(#len(s))
    if end > n:
        end = n
    i32 limit = at_col + max_cols
    i32 c = at_col
    if c < 0:
        c = 0
    if at < 0:
        at = 0
    i32 start = at
    bool moved = false
    for at < end && c < limit:
        for at < end && c < limit:
            i32 b = i32(u8(s[at]))
            if b < PREWARM_FIRST || b > PREWARM_LAST:
                break
            if at + 1 < n && u8(s[at + 1]) >= 0x80:
                break
            u16 slot = g_atlas.ascii[b - PREWARM_FIRST]
            if slot == BLANK_SLOT:
                break
            poke(col + c - at_col, row, slot, 0, fg, bg)
            g_ascii_cells = g_ascii_cells + 1
            at = at + 1
            c = c + 1
            moved = true
        if at >= end || c >= limit:
            break
        if layout(s, at, c, limit, spans[0..<1]) == 0:
            break
        Span sp = spans[0]
        i32 x = col + sp.col - at_col
        if sp.kind == SpanKind.Blank:
            for j in 0..<sp.cols:
                poke(x + j, row, BLANK_SLOT, 0, fg, bg)
        else if sp.cols == 2:
            u16 left = slot_of(s, sp.at, sp.end)
            u16 right = BLANK_SLOT
            if left != NOTDEF_SLOT && left != BLANK_SLOT:
                right = left + 1
            poke(x, row, left, cell.WIDE_LEFT, fg, bg)
            poke(x + 1, row, right, cell.WIDE_RIGHT, fg, bg)
        else:
            poke(x, row, slot_of(s, sp.at, sp.end), 0, fg, bg)
        at = sp.end
        c = sp.col + sp.cols
        moved = true
    if !moved:
        return (start, at_col)
    return (at, c)

// Upload the atlas page if it changed. Outside the render pass, once per
// frame: a second call before the next `begin` does nothing, and a page
// dirtied after the upload goes up next frame. A page that grew since the
// last upload first gets a new image of its new size; if the device refuses
// that image, the old one keeps the rows it has room for (the page's first
// rows, since a grown page is its old self with rows added below), and the
// slots below them sample nothing. A refused size is remembered and not asked
// for again: the page is uploaded once more into the old image, and after that
// it counts as clean (`atlas_dirty` answers false) until a glyph changes it
// again — a refusal that retried every frame would re-upload the page, and
// have the frame redraw, on every frame for ever. A page that grows past the
// refused size tries once more.
void prepare():
    if !g_up || g_page_sent || !g_atlas.page.dirty:
        return
    if g_atlas.page.w != g_img_w || g_atlas.page.h != g_img_h:
        bool refused_before = g_atlas.page.w == g_refused_w && g_atlas.page.h == g_refused_h
        if !refused_before && !make_page_image(g_atlas.page.w, g_atlas.page.h):
            g_error = "grid: the grown atlas page image was refused"
            g_refused_w = g_atlas.page.w
            g_refused_h = g_atlas.page.h
    i64 nbytes = i64(g_img_w) * i64(g_img_h) * i64(texel_bytes(g_atlas.page.format))
    if nbytes < 1 || nbytes > #len(g_atlas.page.pixels):
        return
    sokol_gfx.sg_image_data data
    data.mip_levels[0].ptr = rawptr(&g_atlas.page.pixels[0])
    data.mip_levels[0].size = #c.size_t(nbytes)
    sokol_gfx.sg_update_image(g_img, &data)
    // Still dirty only when the image is short of the page for a reason a
    // later frame can fix; a size already refused is not one.
    g_atlas.page.dirty = g_img_h != g_atlas.page.h && !(g_atlas.page.w == g_refused_w && g_atlas.page.h == g_refused_h)
    g_page_sent = true
    g_page_uploads = g_page_uploads + 1
    g_page_upload_bytes = g_page_upload_bytes + nbytes
    g_frame_page_bytes = nbytes

// Draw the screen: inside the pass, layer by layer from `BASE` up, each as
// its chrome (sokol_gp), then its cells (one instanced draw at the
// framebuffer's origin), then its regions in creation order (one scissored
// instanced draw each; see the file's head), then its ink (sokol_gp, one
// scissor a list; `draw_inks`). A layer's instance buffer is uploaded whole once per
// frame (a second `render` before the next `begin` draws what is already
// there) and remade larger when the screen outgrew it; a layer above the base
// that has not been written since its last clear is neither uploaded nor
// drawn. Nothing is drawn before `up`, for an empty screen, or for a
// non-positive framebuffer.
void render(i32 fb_w, i32 fb_h):
    g_upload_bytes = 0
    g_draw_calls = 0
    g_region_draws = 0
    g_region_uploads = 0
    g_region_upload_bytes = 0
    g_layers_drawn = 0
    g_chrome_tris = 0
    g_chrome_failures = 0
    g_chrome_dropped = 0
    g_chrome_flushes = 0
    ink_frame_reset()
    i32 n = g_cols * g_rows
    if !g_up || g_layers[BASE].cells == nil || n < 1 || fb_w < 1 || fb_h < 1:
        return

    [16]f32 u = [f32(fb_w), f32(fb_h), 0.0, 0.0,
                 f32(g_cw), f32(g_ch), f32(g_cols), f32(g_atlas.slots.across),
                 f32(g_img_w), f32(g_img_h), 0.0, 0.0,
                 f32(g_ul_row), f32(g_strike_row), f32(g_line_thick), 0.0]
    sokol_gfx.sg_range ur
    ur.ptr = rawptr(&u[0])
    ur.size = #c.size_t(64)

    for i in 0..<g_nlayers:
        ^CellLayer l = &g_layers[i]
        if l.chrome.n > 0:
            i32 left = chrome_frame_tris() - g_chrome_frame_used
            if left < 0:
                left = 0
            ChromeDrawn d = chrome_draw(&l.chrome, fb_w, fb_h, left)
            g_chrome_frame_used = g_chrome_frame_used + d.tris
            g_chrome_tris = g_chrome_tris + d.tris
            g_chrome_dropped = g_chrome_dropped + d.dropped
            g_chrome_flushes = g_chrome_flushes + d.flushes
            if d.failed:
                g_chrome_failures = g_chrome_failures + 1
        if layer_draws(i32(i), l.touched) && l.cells != nil && upload_layer(l, n):
            // Applied afresh for every layer: the chrome just drawn went
            // through sokol_gp's own pipeline and bindings.
            draw_cells(l.buf, &ur, n)
            g_draw_calls = g_draw_calls + 1
            g_layers_drawn = g_layers_drawn + 1
        draw_regions(i32(i), fb_w, fb_h, u)
        draw_inks(i32(i), fb_w, fb_h)

// One instanced draw of `n` cells from `buf` with the grid's pipeline, its
// atlas and the uniforms `ur`.
private void draw_cells(sokol_gfx.sg_buffer buf, ^sokol_gfx.sg_range ur, i32 n):
    sokol_gfx.sg_bindings b
    b.vertex_buffers[0] = buf
    b.views[0] = g_view
    b.samplers[0] = g_smp
    sokol_gfx.sg_apply_pipeline(g_pip)
    sokol_gfx.sg_apply_bindings(&b)
    sokol_gfx.sg_apply_uniforms(0, ur)
    sokol_gfx.sg_draw(0, 6, n)

// The pixel rectangle a region's rect covers in a framebuffer `fb_w` x
// `fb_h`, cut to the framebuffer: `x, y, w, h`, top-left origin, with `w` or
// `h` 0 when nothing of it is on screen. Pure. The products are `i64`, since
// a rect's column may be anywhere in `i32`.
(i32 x, i32 y, i32 w, i32 h) region_scissor(cell.CellRect r, i32 cw, i32 ch, i32 fb_w, i32 fb_h):
    if cell.is_empty(r) || cw < 1 || ch < 1 || fb_w < 1 || fb_h < 1:
        return (0, 0, 0, 0)
    i64 x0 = i64(r.col) * i64(cw)
    i64 y0 = i64(r.row) * i64(ch)
    i64 x1 = x0 + i64(r.cols) * i64(cw)
    i64 y1 = y0 + i64(r.rows) * i64(ch)
    if x0 < 0:
        x0 = 0
    if y0 < 0:
        y0 = 0
    if x1 > i64(fb_w):
        x1 = i64(fb_w)
    if y1 > i64(fb_h):
        y1 = i64(fb_h)
    if x1 <= x0 || y1 <= y0:
        return (0, 0, 0, 0)
    return (i32(x0), i32(y0), i32(x1 - x0), i32(y1 - y0))

// Draw `layer`'s regions in creation order, each under a scissor of its rect
// with the grid's origin at the rect's pixel origin less `(0, dy)`. A region
// wholly off the framebuffer is neither uploaded nor drawn; its changes wait
// in its dirty flag.
private void draw_regions(i32 layer, i32 fb_w, i32 fb_h, [16]f32 u):
    for k in 0..<g_region_live:
        ^Region g = &g_regions[g_region_order[k]]
        if g.layer != layer || g.cells == nil:
            continue
        (i32 sx, i32 sy, i32 sw, i32 sh) = region_scissor(g.rect, g_cw, g_ch, fb_w, fb_h)
        if sw < 1 || sh < 1:
            continue
        i32 count = g.rect.cols * (g.rect.rows + 1)
        if !upload_region(g, count):
            continue
        // The rect is on screen, so its origin is within a rect's extent
        // (`MAX_REGION_SIDE` cells) of the framebuffer: far below 2^24,
        // where an `f32` stops holding whole pixels.
        [16]f32 v = u
        v[2] = f32(g.rect.col * g_cw)
        v[3] = f32(g.rect.row * g_ch - g.dy)
        v[6] = f32(g.rect.cols)
        sokol_gfx.sg_range vr
        vr.ptr = rawptr(&v[0])
        vr.size = #c.size_t(64)
        sokol_gfx.sg_apply_scissor_rect(sx, sy, sw, sh, true)
        draw_cells(g.buf, &vr, count)
        sokol_gfx.sg_apply_scissor_rect(0, 0, fb_w, fb_h, true)
        g_region_draws = g_region_draws + 1

// Send a layer's `n` cells to its instance buffer, once a frame, remaking the
// buffer when the screen outgrew it. Answers whether the buffer holds them.
private bool upload_layer(^CellLayer l, i32 n):
    if n > l.buf_cells:
        if !make_buffer(l, n):
            return false
        l.buf_sent = false
    if !l.buf_sent:
        sokol_gfx.sg_range data
        data.ptr = rawptr(&l.cells[0])
        data.size = #c.size_t(u64(n) * u64(cell.CELL_BYTES))
        sokol_gfx.sg_update_buffer(l.buf, &data)
        l.buf_sent = true
        g_upload_bytes = g_upload_bytes + i64(n) * i64(cell.CELL_BYTES)
    return true

// Send a region's `n` cells to its instance buffer when they changed since
// the last upload, remaking the buffer when the rect outgrew it (a fresh
// buffer holds nothing, so it is sent whatever the flag said). At most once
// a frame, like a layer's: cells changed after the upload go up next frame.
private bool upload_region(^Region g, i32 n):
    if n > g.buf_cells:
        if !make_instances(&g.buf, &g.buf_cells, n, false):
            return false
        g.buf_sent = false
        g.dirty = true
    if g.dirty && !g.buf_sent:
        i64 bytes = i64(n) * i64(cell.CELL_BYTES)
        sokol_gfx.sg_range data
        data.ptr = rawptr(&g.cells[0])
        data.size = #c.size_t(bytes)
        sokol_gfx.sg_update_buffer(g.buf, &data)
        g.buf_sent = true
        g.dirty = false
        g.uploads = g.uploads + 1
        g.upload_bytes = g.upload_bytes + bytes
        g_region_uploads = g_region_uploads + 1
        g_region_upload_bytes = g_region_upload_bytes + bytes
    return true

// ── Scrolling regions ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

// The cells a region's writers reach: the rect and the spare row below it.
// Empty for an empty rect. Pure.
cell.CellRect region_extent(cell.CellRect r):
    if cell.is_empty(r):
        return cell.rect(0, 0, 0, 0)
    return cell.rect(r.col, r.row, r.cols, r.rows + 1)

// The clip a region's writers are held to while `clip` is set: `clip` grown
// by one row at the bottom when its bottom edge is the rect's, so that a
// clip set to the rect — or to anything reaching its last row — also admits
// the spare row; otherwise `clip` unchanged. Pure.
cell.CellRect region_clip_for(cell.CellRect clip, cell.CellRect r):
    if cell.is_empty(clip) || cell.is_empty(r):
        return clip
    if clip.row + clip.rows == r.row + r.rows:
        return cell.rect(clip.col, clip.row, clip.cols, clip.rows + 1)
    return clip

private i32 region_capacity():
    if g_regions == nil:
        return 0
    return i32(#len(g_regions))

// Whether `id` names a live region.
bool region_valid(i32 id):
    return id >= 0 && id < region_capacity() && g_regions[id].live

// Live regions.
i32 region_count():
    return g_region_live

// Double the region table and the order with it (four to start), the new
// entries dead. Answers false, having changed neither's length past what
// both hold, when an allocation failed.
private bool grow_regions():
    i32 cap = region_capacity()
    i32 want = cap * 2
    if want < 4:
        want = 4
    if g_regions == nil:
        g_regions = #make([]Region, i64(want), g_alloc)
        if g_regions == nil:
            return false
    else if !#resize(g_regions, i64(want)):
        return false
    Region none
    for i in cap..<want:
        g_regions[i] = none
    if g_region_order == nil:
        g_region_order = #make([]i32, i64(want), g_alloc)
        if g_region_order == nil:
            return false
    else if i32(#len(g_region_order)) < want:
        if !#resize(g_region_order, i64(want)):
            return false
    return true

// A new region on `layer`, with an empty rect and offset 0; answers its id,
// or -1 for a layer that does not exist, before `up` (whose allocator the
// table lives in), and when the table could not grow. A freed id is reused.
i32 region_make(i32 layer):
    if layer < 0 || layer >= g_nlayers || !g_have_alloc:
        return -1
    i32 cap = region_capacity()
    i32 id = -1
    for i in 0..<cap:
        if !g_regions[i].live:
            id = i32(i)
            break
    if id < 0 || g_region_order == nil || g_region_live >= i32(#len(g_region_order)):
        if !grow_regions():
            return -1
        if id < 0:
            id = cap
    Region fresh
    fresh.live = true
    fresh.layer = layer
    g_regions[id] = fresh
    g_region_order[g_region_live] = id
    g_region_live = g_region_live + 1
    return id

// Give back a region's cells and buffer; its id is then free for reuse.
private void release_region(^Region g):
    if g.cells != nil:
        #delete(g.cells)
        g.cells = nil
    if g.buf.id != 0:
        if sokol_gfx.sg_isvalid():
            sokol_gfx.sg_destroy_buffer(g.buf)
        g.buf.id = 0
    Region none
    g^ = none

// Free region `id`; a bad id does nothing. A writer still aimed at it writes
// nowhere — the aim is moved to no region (`targeted_region` answers -1), so
// a later `region_make` that reuses the id is not written through it.
void region_free(i32 id):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return
    if g_region_targeted && g_target_region == id:
        g_target_region = -1
    release_region(&g_regions[id])
    i32 j = 0
    for k in 0..<g_region_live:
        if g_region_order[k] != id:
            g_region_order[j] = g_region_order[k]
            j = j + 1
    g_region_live = j

// Every region, and the table itself: `down`'s.
private void release_regions():
    for i in 0..<region_capacity():
        release_region(&g_regions[i])
    if g_regions != nil:
        #delete(g_regions)
        g_regions = nil
    if g_region_order != nil:
        #delete(g_region_order)
        g_region_order = nil
    g_region_live = 0
    g_region_targeted = false

// Set the screen cells region `id` covers. **Answers true when the rect
// changed**: the buffer is then remade blank, `rect.cols` x `(rect.rows +
// 1)`, and the caller repaints it. The same rect again answers false and keeps
// the cells. An empty rect, or one wider or taller than `MAX_REGION_SIDE`,
// leaves the region with no cells, drawing and taking nothing; so does a
// failed allocation, after which `region_rect` answers the empty rect and the
// same call tries again. A bad id answers false.
bool region_set_rect(i32 id, cell.CellRect r):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return false
    ^Region g = &g_regions[id]
    cell.CellRect want = r
    if cell.is_empty(r) || r.cols > MAX_REGION_SIDE || r.rows > MAX_REGION_SIDE:
        want = cell.rect(0, 0, 0, 0)
    if want == g.rect:
        return false
    if g.cells != nil:
        #delete(g.cells)
        g.cells = nil
    g.rect = cell.rect(0, 0, 0, 0)
    g.dirty = false
    if !cell.is_empty(want):
        g.cells = #make([]cell.Cell, i64(want.cols) * i64(want.rows + 1), g_alloc)
        if g.cells != nil:
            g.rect = want
            g.dirty = true
    return true

// Draw region `id`'s content shifted up by `dy` pixels, held to 0 ..
// `cell_h() - 1` (0 before `up`). Uploads nothing: the offset is a uniform.
void region_set_offset(i32 id, i32 dy):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return
    i32 d = dy
    if d > g_ch - 1:
        d = g_ch - 1
    if d < 0:
        d = 0
    g_regions[id].dy = d

// Shift region `id`'s buffer up by `n` rows (down for `n` < 0): one
// `memmove` of the rows that stay, and the `|n|` rows exposed at the far edge
// made blank — slot 0, transparent both ways — for the caller to paint. `|n|`
// at or past the buffer's `rows + 1` rows clears it all. The rows counted are
// `|n|` held to `rows + 1`; the bytes moved are the rows that stay times the
// row's bytes.
void region_scroll_rows(i32 id, i32 n):
    if !region_valid(id) || n == 0:
        return
    ^Region g = &g_regions[id]
    if g.cells == nil:
        return
    i64 cols = i64(g.rect.cols)
    i64 total = i64(g.rect.rows) + 1
    i64 m = i64(n)
    if m < 0:
        m = 0 - m
    if m > total:
        m = total
    i64 keep = (total - m) * cols
    cell.Cell none = cell.blank()
    if keep > 0:
        i64 bytes = keep * i64(cell.CELL_BYTES)
        if n > 0:
            _ = memmove(rawptr(&g.cells[0]), rawptr(&g.cells[m * cols]), u64(bytes))
            for i in keep..<(total * cols):
                g.cells[i] = none
        else:
            _ = memmove(rawptr(&g.cells[m * cols]), rawptr(&g.cells[0]), u64(bytes))
            for i in 0..<(m * cols):
                g.cells[i] = none
        g.bytes_moved = g.bytes_moved + bytes
    else:
        for i in 0..<(total * cols):
            g.cells[i] = none
    g.rows_shifted = g.rows_shifted + m
    g_region_rows_shifted = g_region_rows_shifted + m
    g.dirty = true

// Point every writer at region `id`, in screen cells (see the file's head).
// A bad id is accepted and writes nowhere. `target` or `begin` points them
// back at a layer.
void target_region(i32 id):
    seg_switch(region_valid(id) && g_regions[id].layer == BASE)
    g_region_targeted = true
    g_target_region = id

// The writers are being pointed at the base (`base`) or elsewhere: close the
// run of cells written since the last switch into the base's count if it was
// the base's.
private void seg_switch(bool base):
    if g_seg_base:
        g_base_cells = g_base_cells + (g_cells_written - g_seg_from)
    g_seg_from = g_cells_written
    g_seg_base = base

// The region the writers write, or -1 while they write a layer.
i32 targeted_region():
    if !g_region_targeted:
        return -1
    return g_target_region

// A region's rect (empty for a bad id), offset, and layer (-1 for a bad id).
cell.CellRect region_rect(i32 id):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return cell.rect(0, 0, 0, 0)
    return g_regions[id].rect

i32 region_offset(i32 id):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return 0
    return g_regions[id].dy

i32 region_layer(i32 id):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return -1
    return g_regions[id].layer

// The cell of region `id` at screen cell (`col`, `row`), the spare row
// included; outside its extent, or for a bad id, the blank cell.
cell.Cell region_cell_at(i32 id, i32 col, i32 row):
    if !region_valid(id):
        return cell.blank()
    ^Region g = &g_regions[id]
    if g.cells == nil || !cell.contains(region_extent(g.rect), col, row):
        return cell.blank()
    return g.cells[i64(row - g.rect.row) * i64(g.rect.cols) + i64(col - g.rect.col)]

// What one region has done since it was made: buffer uploads and their
// bytes, rows shifted and bytes moved by `region_scroll_rows`, and whether its
// cells changed since the last upload. Zero for a bad id.
type RegionStats: (i32 uploads, i64 upload_bytes, i64 rows_shifted, i64 bytes_moved, bool dirty)

RegionStats region_stats(i32 id):
    RegionStats none
    if !region_valid(id):
        return none
    ^Region g = &g_regions[id]
    RegionStats r = (g.uploads, g.upload_bytes, g.rows_shifted, g.bytes_moved, g.dirty)
    return r
